# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values.
#   PowerShell:  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored; this example is committed so anyone setting the
# project up knows which variables exist.
#
# NOTE ON NAMING: this is a Flask/Python app, not Next.js - there is no
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* convention here, and nothing in this file is exposed to
# the browser. Stripe's *publishable* key isn't needed at all: checkout
# happens on Stripe's own hosted page (see /api/subscribe), which is
# also what keeps this app out of PCI scope.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local AI (required) - the app talks to Ollama on this machine.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434

# Ollama Cloud, for running with no machine of your own.
#
# The cloud serves the identical native API, so pointing OLLAMA_URL at it
# and adding a key is the whole change - the app streams from it exactly
# as it streams from a local install. Cloud model names end in -cloud
# (gpt-oss:120b-cloud and so on) and /api/tags lists whatever your key
# can reach, so the model picker fills itself in.
#
#   OLLAMA_URL=https://ollama.com
#   OLLAMA_API_KEY=<https://ollama.com/settings/keys>
#
# Note this changes what the app can honestly claim: with a remote
# endpoint the channel is labelled "Ollama Cloud" rather than "On this
# machine", because prompts do leave the device.
OLLAMA_API_KEY=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Groq (free, no card) - THE fast channel. Effectively required on a
# host with no GPU, where the alternative is a 7B on shared vCPUs
# answering at about a word per second.
#
# Its free tier is 8,000 TOKENS A MINUTE, per key, shared by everyone
# using the site at once - which is a real ceiling a public site reaches
# on an ordinary afternoon, not a theoretical one. The app handles it
# rather than hitting it: it reads the remaining budget from every
# response and routes to the local model before the limit rather than
# after, so running out degrades the reply instead of failing it. Pro
# accounts get first claim on what is left.
#
#   https://console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Owner-only stats page (optional)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The email you sign in with. Set it and /stats shows signups, usage by
# bay, which model answered and credits consumed - all computed from the
# database, with nothing tracked or logged to produce it.
#
# Leave it blank and /stats returns 404 for everyone, including you. That
# is the safe default: the alternative is a public page listing your
# revenue and usage. Traffic figures (visits, countries, referrers) are
# not here - those live in the Cloudflare dashboard.
ADMIN_EMAIL=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Flask session signing (required before deploying)
# Generate:  python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
SECRET_KEY=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe subscriptions - $1.99/mo Pro plan. OPTIONAL: the app runs fine
# without these, it just can't sell Pro.
#
# IMPORTANT: paste the WHOLE key. A truncated value like "sk_test_51..."
# is worse than leaving this blank - the app would treat billing as
# configured, and your customer would hit "Invalid API Key" at the
# checkout button instead of an honest "not available yet". billing_live()
# screens for obvious placeholders, but don't rely on that.
#
#   1. https://dashboard.stripe.com/register - create the account.
#      Settings -> Payouts: add the bank account that receives money.
#   2. Product catalog -> add product "Pro", recurring price $1.99/month.
#      Copy its Price ID (starts "price_") into STRIPE_PRICE_ID_PRO.
#   3. Developers -> API keys -> copy the SECRET key ("sk_test_" while
#      testing, "sk_live_" in production). It is ~100 characters.
#   4. Webhook, so a completed payment actually flips the account to Pro:
#        Local:     stripe listen --forward-to localhost:5000/api/billing/webhook
#        Deployed:  Developers -> Webhooks -> add endpoint
#                   https://yourdomain/api/billing/webhook
#                   events: checkout.session.completed,
#                           customer.subscription.updated,
#                           customer.subscription.deleted
#      Copy its signing secret ("whsec_") below. Without it the webhook
#      refuses every request - it cannot verify a callback really came
#      from Stripe, and acting on an unverified one would let anyone
#      grant themselves Pro.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_PRO=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google sign-in (optional). Email/password signup works without it.
# https://console.cloud.google.com -> OAuth client ID (Web application),
# authorised redirect URI: http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/auth/google/callback
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image generation (optional key, better pictures)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The default image backend is keyless: Pollinations, which serves SANA
# and needs no account. It caps output at about 0.59 megapixels whatever
# size is asked for, and the app resamples up to the size it promised.
#
# Cloudflare Workers AI runs FLUX.1-schnell on a standing free daily
# allocation, and is used automatically for every picture when both of
# these are set. Account id and a token with the Workers AI permission:
#   https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens
CF_ACCOUNT_ID=
CF_API_TOKEN=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev-only escape hatch. "1" lets an account self-upgrade to Pro with no
# payment, so the plan UI can be exercised before Stripe exists. MUST
# stay unset anywhere other people can reach - it is a free-Pro button.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
ALLOW_MOCK_UPGRADE=

# Set to 1 when served over real HTTPS so session cookies are marked
# Secure. Leave blank for local http, or sign-in breaks.
FORCE_HTTPS_COOKIES=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Set to 1 ONLY when something else terminates connections in front
# of this app - a Cloudflare tunnel, nginx, Render's router. It makes
# Flask trust X-Forwarded-Proto/Host, which Google sign-in needs to
# build a redirect_uri matching the one registered in Cloud Console.
# With no proxy in front, leave it unset: those headers would then be
# attacker-controlled.
TRUST_PROXY=

# --- Paddle (subscriptions) --------------------------------------------
# Use these INSTEAD of the Stripe keys above if you are selling from a
# country Stripe does not support - Morocco among them. Paddle is a
# merchant of record: they are legally the seller, handle VAT/sales tax
# worldwide, and pay out to any non-sanctioned country.
#
# When PADDLE_API_KEY and PADDLE_PRICE_ID_PRO are both set, the app uses
# Paddle for checkout and ignores Stripe.
#
# Sandbox and production are separate systems with separate keys, prices
# and dashboards - a sandbox price id is meaningless in production.
PADDLE_ENV=sandbox
PADDLE_API_KEY=
PADDLE_PRICE_ID_PRO=
PADDLE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Browser-side token. Unlike the API key this one is meant to be
# public - it ships inside the page. Paddle's checkout is an overlay
# drawn by Paddle.js rather than a page to redirect to, and Paddle.js
# will not start without it, so Upgrade silently does nothing.
# Paddle > Developer tools > Authentication > Client-side tokens.
PADDLE_CLIENT_TOKEN=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Video generation (optional) - PixVerse.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ONLY feature here that spends real money per use: roughly $0.13
# for a five-second clip through a reseller, up to $0.15/sec direct.
# There is no free tier - entry is $100/mo or $10 for ~22 clips.
#
# So it is Pro-only and capped at a hard 10 clips per calendar month,
# counted in its own table rather than against credits: credits refill
# on a timer and this cost does not. Leave blank and the video bay
# shows as not set up, costing nothing.
#   https://platform.pixverse.ai
PIXVERSE_API_KEY=

# Free-tier video generation (hfvideo.py).
# A free Hugging Face account, no card. Create a FINE-GRAINED token
# with the "Make calls to Inference Providers" permission:
#   https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens
#
# Used only when PIXVERSE_API_KEY is absent - paid first when it is
# set, since someone who paid should get what they paid for. The
# free allowance is monthly and sized for evaluation, so the bay
# tells people when it runs out rather than showing an error.
HF_TOKEN=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3D model generation (tripo3d.py)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The video bay becomes a 3D bay when this is set.
#
# NOT FREE. A search result claimed new accounts get 2,000 credits;
# a real new account returns {"balance": 0}. It is the CHEAPEST of the
# generative bays - roughly $0.20 a model against $0.45 for a PixVerse
# clip - but it needs a funded account, and the bay says so rather than
# failing on the first click.
#   https://platform.tripo3d.ai
TRIPO_API_KEY=

# Kimi, via OpenRouter. THIS ONE COSTS MONEY PER MESSAGE - roughly half a
# cent for a coding turn - and it is the only model in the app that does.
# Leave it unset and the code bay stays on free gpt-oss exactly as before;
# set it and Kimi becomes the coding model. Groq serves no Moonshot model
# at all, which is why this is a separate provider.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Email verification. Without these the six-digit code is printed to the
# server log instead of sent - the flow works either way, only the
# delivery changes. Gmail needs an app password, not your login password.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=
